
    <!-- What Can It Do Section -->
    <div id="what-can-it-do" class="section">
        <div class="container">
        <div class="row align-items-center feature-row">
            <div class="col-md-5 mb-3 mb-md-0">
                <div class="code-card">
                    <pre>
$ {{#cb}}foks{{/cb}} git create secrets
Created: foks://acme.co/alice/secrets

$ {{#cb}}git{{/cb}} remote add origin foks://acme.co/alice/secrets

$ {{#cb}}git{{/cb}} push origin main
&#x23FB; Initializing FOKS ... &#x2705; done
&#x261D;&#xFE0F; Indexing packfiles ... &#x2705; done
&#x2B06;&#xFE0F; Pushing objects to remote ... &#x2705; done
To foks://acme.co/alice/secrets
 * [new branch]      main -> main</pre>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-7">
                <h3>End-to-End Post-Quantum Encrypted Git Hosting</h3>
                <p>
                    FOKS provides a secure, end-to-end encrypted Git hosting service.
                    Your data is encrypted on your machine before it hits the server,
                    and the server never sees data or filenames in unencrypted form.
                    FOKS uses PQ-secure public-key cryptography, securing all end-to-end
                    encrypted data against future quantum attacks.
                    Your secrets are safe, even in the event of server compromise.
                </p>
                <p>
                    What's more, FOKS is federated, so the git server can be one that
                    you host, or one that is hosted for you.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="row align-items-center feature-row">
            <div class="col-md-5 mb-3 mb-md-0">
                <div class="code-card">
                    <pre>
$ {{#cb}}foks{{/cb}} kv put /secrets/vitals/ssn 123-45-6789
$ {{#cb}}foks{{/cb}} kv get /secrets/vitals/ssn -
123-45-6789

$ {{#cb}}foks{{/cb}} kv put -f /secrets/doc.pdf ~/doc.pdf
$ {{#cb}}foks{{/cb}} kv get /secrets/doc.pdf /tmp/doc.pdf
$ {{#cb}}diff{{/cb}} ~/doc.pdf /tmp/doc.pdf && echo "the same"
the same</pre>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-7">
                <h3>End-to-End Post-Quantum Encrypted Key-Value Store</h3>
                <p>
                    Internally, FOKS implements git atop an encrypted key-value store.
                    Clients encrypt keys and values before sending them up to the server,
                    and decrypt them upon retrieval. You can access
                    this key-value store directly, which can store either
                    short strings, or large data files.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="row align-items-center feature-row">
            <div class="col-md-5 mb-3 mb-md-0">
                <div class="code-card">
                    <pre>
$ {{#cb}}foks{{/cb}} team list-memberships
owner    devops
member   marketing@widgets.org
member   seo@gizmos.net  (via marketing@widgets.org)

$ {{#cb}}foks{{/cb}} team list devops
owner    alice
admin    bob
member   charlie@widgets.org
member   t:devops@gizmos.net</pre>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-7">
                <h3>Federated and Generalized Team Management</h3>
                <p>
                    FOKS provides a federated and generalized team management system.
                    You can create teams, add members, and assign roles.
                    You can also delegate team management to other team members.
                    Teams can be members of other teams, allowing for complex
                    team topologies that mirror real-world organizations.
                    Moreover, teams can cross federated boundaries, allowing
                    users on different FOKS servers to share data with end-to-end encryption.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="row align-items-center feature-row">
            <div class="col-md-5 mb-3 mb-md-0">
                <div class="code-card">
                    <pre>
$ {{#cb}}foks{{/cb}} key list
* laptop 2024-05-04    computer      active
  neither 3213         backup        active
  YubiKey 5 NFC        yubikey       active
  desktop 2023-04-01   computer      revoked</pre>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-7">
                <h3>Device Management with Full YubiKey Support</h3>
                <p>
                    FOKS provides a device management system with full YubiKey support.
                    You can register your devices with FOKS and manage their access.
                    You can also revoke access to a device if it is lost or stolen.
                    Revoking triggers a cascading key-rotation across all teams you are members of,
                    whether local or federated. This keeps data secure going forward.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="row align-items-center feature-row">
            <div class="col-md-5 mb-3 mb-md-0">
                <div class="code-card">
                    <pre>
# agent stores encrypted memory &amp; artifacts
$ {{#cb}}foks{{/cb}} kv put /agents/scratch/run-2387 "$({{#cb}}cat{{/cb}} memo.json)"
$ {{#cb}}foks{{/cb}} kv get /agents/scratch/run-2387 -
{"next":"deploy-staging", ...}

# scoped team membership alongside humans
$ {{#cb}}foks{{/cb}} team add devops ci-runner --role member

# one command decommissions the agent
$ {{#cb}}foks{{/cb}} key list
* ci-runner            agent         revoked
  laptop 2026-04-01    computer      active</pre>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-7">
                <h3>A Secure Substrate for AI Agents</h3>
                <p>
                    Like humans, agents need credentials, persistent memory, and access to code.
                    FOKS gives each agent its own first-class identity,
                    so it can read and write encrypted memory in the KV store, push
                    work to end-to-end encrypted Git repos, and join teams under scoped roles.
                </p>
                <p>
                    When an agent is retired or compromised, a single key revocation
                    cascades a full key rotation across every team it touched.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        </div>
    </div>
